From 0fdc12878d1038f13a017b994fae89b1672b3fb0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 18:45:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(ui): render authored Bedrock server forms

Render the core-resolved resource-pack form scene through a negotiated
client payload, preserving the original form ID and button indices. Use
accepted pack textures and bundled licensed UI definitions without
starting account sign-in or another package acquisition when a form opens.

Keep authored control hierarchy, anchors, content dimensions, visibility,
image borders and alpha tracks. Use Java focus, narration and pointer
handling for responses and bounded horizontal or vertical scrolling.
Fall back to the original ordinary form when a visible control or dimension
cannot be rendered. Omit missing or corrupt individual images while
preserving captions and usable responses.

Original native 1.26.51.1/protocol2193 execution binds the complete
visibility setter and dependency scheduler: hidden computed dimensions
reset to zero without removing authored properties or collection entries.
Use those metrics for child and peer sizes, preserve disabled dimensions,
and default stack cross-axis sizes to the parent extent. This prevents
inactive factory templates from consuming title and category-list space.

Render native left/top/right/bottom image borders and signed undersized
regions with metadata-scaled float UVs through the normal Java GUI path.
The bounded layout solver and polynomial alpha easing still need wider
native comparison. Native GPU winding, font metrics, size/UV/color tracks
and named next-animation chains remain explicit parity boundaries.

Load public HTTPS form images asynchronously, with bounded downloads and
decoding, versioned URL caches and texture cleanup. Keep original buttons
usable while images load or fail; never put remote image bytes in the
scene payload or block its network/render thread on HTTP.

Resolve the native hover-text renderer through Java formatted tooltips
without changing response indices or arbitrary custom renderer semantics.
